ducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862dd746-e765-5276-9c3d-a3df22502c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173945} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc658c-f069-51f0-ab2d-d1edef81492e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173946} {:answer/id #uuid "a76729e7-40ac-5002-883b-ca750e2e549d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173947} {:answer/id #uuid "d95e6af2-6a09-532e-b47a-027634bf7fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173948} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fcc32-97fa-52dd-a9a0-5800b1d12609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173949}] :answer-field/id #uuid "31b89bfc-b72f-5819-96e0-040551b0f540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173944}] :question/id #uuid "7e0868ec-2423-5a95-9212-2ae581f51f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-112432" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "511398e8-780e-57b2-8f1b-092e9f6cca45" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Scalar Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6242b1ee-e4b0-599e-84c4-cbf8ececc4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386614} {:answer/id #uuid "382ba324-163c-5309-8590-80f0ad43b8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386615} {:answer/id #uuid "7a782f79-ef65-59ba-b828-5288c783cc45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386616} {:answer/id #uuid "7b67060c-e19d-5a66-afc9-87bb040c5ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386617} {:answer/id #uuid "68cf070d-4024-5e82-b374-b2728845cd05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68cf070d-4024-5e82-b374-