canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=-5I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0156feab-5605-5992-8da3-4738453f6073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386641} {:answer/id #uuid "121b476a-698c-5332-9282-ceda6db87e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386642} {:answer/id #uuid "5147eb99-5244-5a63-8d09-579772e00ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386643} {:answer/id #uuid "00d86800-4577-51bf-b6fc-7222bde42216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386644} {:answer/id #uuid "839cc5ba-8f55-59de-a7ca-dfae41fbeeea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5147eb99-5244-5a63-8d09-579772e00ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386643} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b805227-a935-524e-88be-f669298cff28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58112fb5-e289-5b2d-b171-03f84610dc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-112478" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(9)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c074f1f1-190d-5c2b-be44-47f1519f9e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386648} {:answer/id #uuid "428736dc-252f-56df-b2cf-058df8e2f2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386649} {:answer/id #uuid "4293ab9b-7451-59be-b692-d3049e359a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386650} {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386651} {:answer/id #uuid "dba8598e-a9a8-5938-a8b8-24e380b6f380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386651} :answer-field/id #uuid "570439f3-213e-5838-a79e-eae761e0d96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bcd060e-67aa-5a69-a9d1-f310cdace57a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113105" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be58f37-f54d-5573-8196-1a48e0120402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386655} {:answer/id #uuid "edf6b54e-f03a-5462-82df-86ef6830c52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386656} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc442e-715c-566e-b72b-a51cbea668bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386657} {:answer/id #uuid "1618c4fa-4747-58fd-aa37-8dc8ecfb8811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386658} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc376ce-b4cd-5c79-81d9-356efde6d398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc442e-715c-566e-b72b-a51cbea668bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386657} :answer-field/id #uuid "bffa88cf-8ebd-54fd-8ec7-e9e316961451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75c20b7a-b2e0-5fac-95fd-d5bd3970e423" :question/math-academy-id "q-113167" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x-1}{3{x}^{2}-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x-1}{3{x}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{3-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22044db0-b676-5a41-8839-2fb6643aef6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eeb158823a32a215dcf72d67bd27b8ad5df238ee44af535afd5850a15f9d17c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386662} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc5cd0e-29d2-5786-8b2b-2a6c87539104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fdc3e5ca668f2e66df09049bfa51fbc156160c091164dafa5f217be1bfbb4c27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386663} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef4fb86-1493-59fa-ba65-4676e4d1f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0fe642f3c7873344885b0e030fb449a8bf882120e675f18d72668ccae3ef4b40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386664} {:answer/id #uuid "f202128d-2196-55f0-a2a3-d143c36c2917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c62cf53292f3209edcc632c313d849fe6a93281bb232ee6d8c1af4d106067e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386665} {:answer/id #uuid "259a8f1e-5010-502b-a4b3-dfcf477e4dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c7b719326bfa1a4b53fdf2bce3f6e7f1dafe5de2c994ffb4951a6d79af78707.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22044db0-b676-5a41-8839-2fb6643aef6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eeb158823a32a215dcf72d67bd27b8ad5df238ee44af535afd5850a15f9d17c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386662} :answer-field/id #uuid "52f032a3-4028-58cf-8c68-fdde2aa0f6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3c5f4d7-3c89-56cc-ae5f-817e3519764f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113324" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{5}{x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3a79a8dbe82342c0ab1e16371f8c1d82833f4ebc3bad659f1a619ab1e92733e.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ee/eeb158823a32a215dcf72d67bd27b8ad5df238ee44af535afd5850a15f9d17c0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e11164-1929-594d-9f9e-460f6469421d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Negative Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eece12fb-474d-55c7-9839-0528edd2d59e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e4247c7cea75f1b89cd9bb161a96213de3529ce3c17926adf8633ae9483f154b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386669} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa6b6c2-1227-566c-873c-872acf62b94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c525edcf937bfdc311acd0d7cac778802e1939dc2bd61fd3a5bf58f808712220.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386670} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d1cdf3-c1b6-5a74-884a-d301bbdede4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f00fdde9e08069b5b0253fd2a871bf0c652644a30d38f4b5f09d003c44bc119f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386671} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5c43d3-2fe9-51d5-95a2-65127b8bdaa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/7152303066f322a8179b2e37863cea