d}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde940dc-e573-5e35-8fd2-9c252d6ce798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386758} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea42a36-7b19-591a-a27a-ef6e9d65fa1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386759} {:answer/id #uuid "cd80673b-5cf1-5dab-9df5-c5df1ea7e9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386760} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf42b98-f7a1-5864-8596-4e091c097444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386761} {:answer/id #uuid "1af9ab94-7d78-52be-97cf-e3834599d6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea42a36-7b19-591a-a27a-ef6e9d65fa1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386759} :answer-field/id #uuid "182a2de1-8101-5395-96f9-70402cec0f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8d0f5b-37a6-5193-9c93-2abd275a5141" :question/math-academy-id "q-11487" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=4x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/b2/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=5.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/bf/bf79cec9c1ede4a95b3e20415fbf37224ca8616426e8530043fa4a4b54780bab.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(4x)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
